1
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:25,530
 She knows I live!

2
00:00:25,530 --> 00:01:47,780
 There's a little fishing village in Po-

3
00:01:47,780 --> 00:02:24,700
 Better make the shot!

4
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:25,920
 Eh, you're checking her what?

5
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:35,800
 Steal against the wind!

6
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:45,440
 Size any second, quick!

7
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:47,320
 Move to the other side as a counterweight!

8
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:51,770
 Go!

9
00:02:51,770 --> 00:02:54,990
 The children took my boat and they almost capsized!

10
00:02:54,990 --> 00:02:55,630
 The children!

11
00:02:55,630 --> 00:02:56,290
 My son!

12
00:02:56,290 --> 00:02:58,240
 He was just sleeping in the boat!

13
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:00,520
 That boy really knows what he's doing, look at him!

14
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:02,940
 Vasco will be a good sailor, he's a natural!

15
00:03:02,940 --> 00:03:09,640
 That lad we've just seen proving his seamanship in his native Portuguese village near Lisbon

16
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:12,160
 will very soon go a long way, a very long way.

17
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:14,840
 The end of the 15th century was approaching.

18
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:18,560
 Christopher Columbus, 18 years his senior, would soon set off for America.

19
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:20,670
 Columbus thought he was sailing to the Indies,

20
00:03:20,670 --> 00:03:24,610
 but adventurers were already sailing farther across the sea, and some went by land,

21
00:03:24,610 --> 00:03:28,230
 like a certain Pedro Coriglia, who was sent by the King of Portugal as an emissary

22
00:03:28,230 --> 00:03:34,800
 to spy and travel the so-called spice route: pepper, ginger, cinnamon and cloves, saffron, nutmeg.

23
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:40,300
 At that time, these were very precious and, of course, very expensive.

24
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:44,550
 Expensive. What are you saying? I can go to the supermarket and buy all I want.

25
00:03:44,730 --> 00:03:48,070
 Yes, today you can. But back then, it wasn't quite so simple.

26
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:52,770
 We'll see for yourself. The voyage it required at each port of call, duties were levied and merchants' profits paid.

27
00:03:53,110 --> 00:03:57,800
 And then when the spices finally arrived in Lisbon...

28
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:01,120
 Hey, merchant, I will have some pepper and nutmeg.

29
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:05,020
 No, but sir, prices have gone up. Transportation is higher, not to mention the taxes.

30
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:09,100
 And now it's taxes on taxes. It means the end of shopkeeping as we know it.

31
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:09,680
 How much?

32
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:12,700
 Well, sir, I'm afraid pepper is worth its weight in pure silver.

33
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:14,210
 That's how it is.

34
00:04:14,210 --> 00:04:16,120
 That's sheer madness.

35
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:17,900
 Nevertheless, I need it very well.

36
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:19,650
 Good decision, sir.

37
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:27,960
 A nutmeg is worth half. It's weighed in pure gold.

38
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:30,490
 That's how it is, a luxury product.

39
00:04:30,490 --> 00:04:39,140
 Father, it's a fortune.

40
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:41,150
 Yes, we pay too much for spices.

41
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:41,700
 You're right.

42
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:43,450
 Well, we have our own ships. We could get it.

43
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:44,750
 That's impossible, my boy.

44
00:04:44,750 --> 00:04:47,010
 We don't know where or how to find such spices.

45
00:04:47,010 --> 00:04:50,470
 Some experienced sailors say you'd have to sail all the way around Africa,

46
00:04:50,470 --> 00:04:51,510
 but who would dare try it?

47
00:04:51,510 --> 00:04:53,450
 They say the sea rushes into a void.

48
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:55,190
 That sea monsters lie and wait for ships.

49
00:04:55,310 --> 00:04:57,950
 Why, one of our men has voyaged farthest, Captain Gomez.

50
00:04:58,410 --> 00:05:00,110
 And he barely passed the equator.

51
00:05:00,430 --> 00:05:00,670
 Oh.

52
00:05:01,320 --> 00:05:02,150
 I'm only ten.

53
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:04,150
 Everything will be discovered when I grow up.

54
00:05:04,210 --> 00:05:05,730
 Oh, my son, there will be discoveries.

55
00:05:05,850 --> 00:05:07,990
 No one has reached the tip of Africa yet.

56
00:05:08,230 --> 00:05:09,010
 Or the Indies.

57
00:05:09,270 --> 00:05:10,430
 We really know so little.

58
00:05:10,590 --> 00:05:12,170
 The pole star vanishes in the south.

59
00:05:12,610 --> 00:05:13,010
 Disappears?

60
00:05:13,130 --> 00:05:13,490
 Yes.

61
00:05:13,490 --> 00:05:15,230
 You've heard of the pole star, Vasco?

62
00:05:15,230 --> 00:05:15,790
 Yes, Father.

63
00:05:16,010 --> 00:05:17,910
 Pedro told me all about the big and little dippers.

64
00:05:18,110 --> 00:05:18,430
 Dippers?

65
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:19,070
 What do you mean?

66
00:05:19,270 --> 00:05:21,050
 I'll be very happy to explain about it, Father.

67
00:05:22,050 --> 00:05:24,290
 There's the constellation of the great bear.

68
00:05:24,410 --> 00:05:25,270
 Some call it the Big Dipper.

69
00:05:25,610 --> 00:05:29,050
 You calculate a distance five times as great as the distance between the stars and the handle,

70
00:05:29,230 --> 00:05:31,070
 and that's the pole star, indicating north.

71
00:05:31,350 --> 00:05:34,670
 And the Little Dipper, the little bear, the pole star, is at the end of the handle.

72
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:38,740
 And do you know why it is the pole star disappears when we approach the equator?

73
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:42,110
 You know why? The Earth's round.

74
00:05:42,390 --> 00:05:46,040
 As we go toward the south, it gets lower on the horizon until it disappears.

75
00:05:47,010 --> 00:05:51,380
 Young Vasco da Gama will serve as apprentice seaman.

76
00:05:53,070 --> 00:05:55,010
 He'll take part on many voyages.

77
00:05:57,280 --> 00:06:03,340
 A few years later, his friend, Bartholomew Diaz, will be the first to sail and reach the southern tip of Africa.

78
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:09,160
 The Cape of Storms. Later, it will be called the Cape of Good Hope.

79
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:11,410
 Let's go!

80
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:13,730
 Oh, you reached the Indian Ocean!

81
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:18,220
 Do you realize, Bartholomew, no one's ever gone as far as you did?

82
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:21,380
 I might have gone much farther, but my crew wouldn't go on.

83
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:23,300
 Around Africa, all the way to the Indies.

84
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:25,120
 Who can say that my men were terrified?

85
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:26,980
 Insisted on sailing back, so here I am.

86
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:29,420
 Who's that sailor? Do you know him?

87
00:06:29,790 --> 00:06:31,090
 He's a captain from Genoa.

88
00:06:31,510 --> 00:06:32,970
 His name is Christopher Columbus.

89
00:06:33,530 --> 00:06:37,870
 For some time now, he's tried to convince our king that he can reach the Indies by sailing west.

90
00:06:38,510 --> 00:06:40,290
 My sailing west? That's idiocy.

91
00:06:40,610 --> 00:06:45,010
 If you can reach the Indies by sailing around Africa, you'll ruin all I planned.

92
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:52,340
 This is just what we need.

93
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:54,100
 Come, my friends, look.

94
00:06:54,180 --> 00:06:58,140
 Look at this map. It is highly secret and includes everything known about the world.

95
00:06:58,140 --> 00:07:02,860
 My astronomer, Abraham Zakutu Ben Samuel, has brought it up to date,

96
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:05,700
 including information on Pero de Caviglia on the Indies.

97
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:06,760
 It's quite interesting.

98
00:07:07,120 --> 00:07:09,770
 The Indies? No one's ever sailed to the Indies.

99
00:07:09,770 --> 00:07:12,270
 Caviglia's been all the way to where he made me on a camel.

100
00:07:12,270 --> 00:07:13,590
 And the Arabs let him do it?

101
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:15,990
 They converted him into an Arab.

102
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:21,090
 So once I'd sailed around Africa, I was closer to the Indies than I was to Portugal.

103
00:07:21,090 --> 00:07:24,590
 Unless I'm wrong, you could have reached the Indies in less than a month.

104
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:26,270
 No, my crew wouldn't go on.

105
00:07:26,270 --> 00:07:27,520
 What is this here?

106
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:30,120
 Oh, that's a land discovered by one of my captains.

107
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:31,620
 It's a state secret.

108
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:34,010
 On your oath, you must tell nobody.

109
00:07:34,010 --> 00:07:36,410
 Couldn't that be the Indies?

110
00:07:36,410 --> 00:07:38,520
 Oh, no, it's a newly discovered land.

111
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:43,230
 We found no gold or spices, but there isn't wood as rather as braised coal, so it's Brazil.

112
00:07:43,230 --> 00:07:48,810
 Well then, that captain from Genoa who wants to sail westward to the Indies, what was his name?

113
00:07:48,810 --> 00:07:54,300
 Christopher Columbus. He's wrong. To get to the Indies, you have to sail around Africa to the east.

114
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:59,790
 I see why your majesty refuses to finance his expedition. He's--

115
00:07:59,790 --> 00:08:05,660
 I have confidence in only you, my Portuguese captains. This Columbus is now selling his services to Spain.

116
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:15,410
 On the 12th of October, 1492, after 36 days at sea, Columbus landed.

117
00:08:15,890 --> 00:08:20,130
 He thought he had reached the Indies, but the American continent got in the way.

118
00:08:20,410 --> 00:08:22,150
 He discovered it for the Europeans.

119
00:08:22,790 --> 00:08:26,910
 You could say he didn't know where he was going, he didn't know where he was when he got there,

120
00:08:26,930 --> 00:08:28,470
 and he didn't know where he'd been when he got back.

121
00:08:28,850 --> 00:08:32,230
 Ferdinand and Isabella of Spain have an audience with the Pope, Alexander VI.

122
00:08:32,230 --> 00:08:37,490
 Your Holiness, it is most important that Spanish and Catholic interests be protected over the Western Oceans.

123
00:08:37,830 --> 00:08:40,270
 Permit me to remind you that you are Spanish as well.

124
00:08:40,270 --> 00:08:41,010
 Meh.

125
00:08:41,010 --> 00:08:42,030
 Write it down.

126
00:08:42,030 --> 00:08:47,210
 I would declare that all territories discovered by Christopher Columbus now and henceforth are Spanish.

127
00:08:47,210 --> 00:08:51,450
 All other territories discovered west of the Azores will be considered possessions of Spain as well.

128
00:08:51,450 --> 00:08:52,250
 Will that do it?

129
00:08:52,250 --> 00:08:53,310
 Uh-huh.

130
00:09:06,590 --> 00:09:08,650
 Here's your edict.

131
00:09:08,650 --> 00:09:12,510
 And a parchment with the papal bull sent to the king of Portugal.

132
00:09:12,510 --> 00:09:14,330
 No, I cannot agree with this!

133
00:09:14,330 --> 00:09:17,330
 That's it! They want everything, he's Spanish!

134
00:09:17,330 --> 00:09:22,320
 I will personally confront Ferdinand and Isabella, but give me the other map, I won't take this one.

135
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:31,290
 It is the Pope's decree, he's quite right. Spain is a large country, it's only right that we take the greater part.

136
00:09:31,290 --> 00:09:34,410
 Now Portugal's small with a smaller part, that's all.

137
00:09:34,410 --> 00:09:39,770
 We do not agree. I remind you Portugal has a powerful and well-equipped navy. Consider.

138
00:09:39,770 --> 00:09:41,130
 What do you suggest?

139
00:09:41,130 --> 00:09:43,710
 I suggest a line of demarcation drawn here.

140
00:09:43,710 --> 00:09:45,570
 I don't see what you gain.

141
00:09:45,570 --> 00:09:49,410
 There's nothing but water. However, if that will please you.

142
00:09:49,410 --> 00:09:54,520
 And so you see, through the treaty signed at Tordesales, Portugal acquired Brazil.

143
00:09:54,520 --> 00:09:57,630
 The Spanish didn't even know it existed, but the Portuguese did.

144
00:09:57,630 --> 00:09:59,930
 Ah...

145
00:09:59,930 --> 00:10:08,190
 You've made up my mind, Vasco, you will command the exposition to the Indies.

146
00:10:08,190 --> 00:10:12,990
 Paulo de Gama, you will be captain of one of the ships.

147
00:10:12,990 --> 00:10:19,100
 And you, Abraham, you will instruct my captains in the mysteries of the stars in the southern hemisphere.

148
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:24,870
 Yes, with your knowledge and experience, you're the best suited to supervise preparations.

149
00:10:24,870 --> 00:10:27,670
 You'll navigate by the Southern Cross, Captains.

150
00:10:27,670 --> 00:10:35,580
 Manuel, my cousin, you'll soon be king. You must always remember the interests of Portugal lie beyond the seas.

151
00:10:35,580 --> 00:10:42,260
 Salted meat, biscuits, rumpel, sardines, prunes, almonds, onions, garlic, and mustard.

152
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:44,700
 Sugar, flour, honey, it's all here.

153
00:10:44,700 --> 00:11:01,490
 Sign to the supply ship. I'm gonna give 'em plenty of headaches, all right.

154
00:11:01,490 --> 00:11:04,210
 And I'm gonna make sure that they get a big reception in Africa.

155
00:11:04,590 --> 00:11:07,670
 Don't worry, the Spanish always pay well, not for you.

156
00:11:08,110 --> 00:11:14,260
 The Holy Church grants eternal salvation to all those who boldly venture forth on the seas for the glory of our country.

157
00:11:21,670 --> 00:11:22,150
 Starboard!

158
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:24,000
 Close heart of starboard!

159
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:39,210
 If you sail along the coast, your route will be shorter and safer.

160
00:11:39,530 --> 00:11:42,140
 But the winds are erratic, and the currents will slow you up.

161
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:43,220
 I won't hug the coast.

162
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:46,520
 I'm going much farther out and due south to take advantage of favorable winds.

163
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:48,280
 A thousand miles out to sea?

164
00:11:48,580 --> 00:11:48,850
 Hmm.

165
00:11:49,330 --> 00:11:52,090
 Well, it will be dangerous, but you know what you're doing.

166
00:11:52,090 --> 00:11:53,280
 May God protect you.

167
00:11:53,280 --> 00:13:00,080
 Polestar, also called the North Star.

168
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:02,270
 You will go below the horizon. We're near the equator.

169
00:13:02,270 --> 00:13:05,360
 But then, find directions, Captain.

170
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:06,710
 Ha! You'll see, lad.

171
00:13:06,710 --> 00:13:07,890
 What is your name?

172
00:13:07,890 --> 00:13:10,110
 Miguelito. I want to be a sailor one day.

173
00:13:10,110 --> 00:13:15,770
 Here, of course, South, South, West.

174
00:13:15,770 --> 00:13:17,650
 Was it South, South, West, Captain?

175
00:13:17,650 --> 00:13:18,670
 Oh, well...

176
00:13:18,670 --> 00:13:21,720
 You heard me. I want South, South, West.

177
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:25,550
 Harder star!

178
00:13:25,550 --> 00:13:29,020
 Get it.

179
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:30,860
 It's Wazzy steering right or left.

180
00:13:30,860 --> 00:13:31,620
 Starboard?

181
00:13:31,620 --> 00:13:33,980
 Not very simple. Starboard is to the right of a ship.

182
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:36,830
 Starboard is to the right and port to the left of the ship.

183
00:13:37,150 --> 00:13:41,930
 Right, but why to starboard to the right, when Africa is on the left to port?

184
00:13:41,930 --> 00:13:46,410
 It's a daring decision, but based upon observations of Portuguese sailors who'd sail these waters,

185
00:13:46,410 --> 00:13:53,730
 Vasco made for open sea to avoid the counter currents and unfavorable winds that he knew he would find that were nearer to the coast.

186
00:13:53,730 --> 00:13:57,760
 Six weeks out on the open sea, the captain's taken us to our doom!

187
00:13:57,760 --> 00:13:59,640
 Yes, we oughta go back home!

188
00:13:59,640 --> 00:14:01,180
 We could come and do this ship!

189
00:14:01,180 --> 00:14:03,260
 Yeah, let's do it!

190
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:12,730
 Hmm, you see, the midday sun's practically below the horizon, we are near the Tropic of Capricorn.

191
00:14:12,730 --> 00:14:13,510
 Yeah, right?

192
00:14:13,650 --> 00:14:14,460
 Here.

193
00:14:14,460 --> 00:14:16,770
 So we sail to port side, close to the east?

194
00:14:16,770 --> 00:14:18,170
 That's right, Miguelito!

195
00:14:18,170 --> 00:14:18,970
 Relay my order.

196
00:14:18,970 --> 00:14:19,470
 Go on.

197
00:14:19,470 --> 00:14:20,160
 Me, sir?

198
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:21,080
 Yes.

199
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:21,730
 Haha.

200
00:14:21,730 --> 00:14:25,830
 Harder port side, steer due east!

201
00:14:25,830 --> 00:14:28,840
 Giving the order!

202
00:14:28,840 --> 00:14:29,940
 Those are my orders.

203
00:14:29,940 --> 00:14:31,880
 Harder port side, steer due east!

204
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:35,010
 Hi Captain, at your orders!

205
00:14:35,010 --> 00:14:39,550
 Meanwhile, on the other side of Africa...

206
00:14:39,550 --> 00:14:42,980
 Isn't very fine to Mombasa.

207
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:44,280
 Only three, four weeks.

208
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:45,610
 Who's in hurry hurry?

209
00:14:45,610 --> 00:14:51,540
 Hmm.

210
00:14:51,540 --> 00:14:54,520
 Not since we've been out to sea and no side of land.

211
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:56,120
 With this Captain, we'll all be lost to-

212
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:02,030
 Now we take the ship!

213
00:15:02,030 --> 00:15:05,790
 That thing!

214
00:15:05,790 --> 00:15:06,390
 Seaweed!

215
00:15:06,390 --> 00:15:07,850
 We must be near land!

216
00:15:07,850 --> 00:15:08,550
 Sound the depth.

217
00:15:12,460 --> 00:15:12,560
 104, 105, 106, 107, 108, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109, 109,

218
00:15:12,460 --> 00:15:21,640
 104, 105, 106, 107, 108, 109, 1010, 9, 7, 6, 7, 8.

219
00:15:21,640 --> 00:15:24,680
 Remarkable, truly extraordinary feat. Never done till then.

220
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,320
 Three months out in the open sea with no means of navigation except the stars.

221
00:15:28,660 --> 00:15:32,000
 And Vasco da Gama stepped ashore almost exactly where it intended.

222
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:34,720
 Not far from the Cape of Good Hope, naturally.

223
00:15:34,980 --> 00:15:36,640
 The sailors shouted with joy.

224
00:15:36,980 --> 00:15:48,940
 Yay. The last of me.

225
00:15:49,040 --> 00:16:01,180
 Stay here a few days to repair our sails and then we'll take on our new provisions.

226
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:38,090
 See to it. Turn to his home. Take you ashore.

227
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:44,700
 Seem friendly and very much like us.

228
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:47,360
 I think that we might get on very well, all of us, don't you think?

229
00:16:48,230 --> 00:16:53,530
 No. The Portuguese are very bad. They're bad.

230
00:16:54,220 --> 00:16:56,260
 Watch your necklaces, and your bracelet.

231
00:16:56,930 --> 00:16:59,820
 You've got our assing eyes, your spears, right?

232
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:01,190
 Oh, I'm good. I'm good.

233
00:17:01,750 --> 00:17:02,910
 Yeah, yeah, yeah.

234
00:17:09,260 --> 00:17:21,770
 But I am surprised. They seem to be extremely friendly.

235
00:17:22,210 --> 00:17:26,080
 Well, too bad. We have our provisions, and so we can be on our way.

236
00:17:26,080 --> 00:17:27,100
 We sail south.

237
00:17:33,910 --> 00:17:37,600
 There. The exact spot where Bartholomew Diaz turned around ten years ago.

238
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:40,660
 You mean nobody's ever gone any, well, any further?

239
00:17:40,660 --> 00:18:02,760
 Not yet.

240
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:49,570
 Supply!

241
00:18:49,570 --> 00:18:54,140
 Are you going to come here with a lot of Portuguese sailors?

242
